>     Reviewed-by: Josh Triplett <josh@joshtriplett.org>

I re-reviewed this commit, and I am not seeing how it could cause these
results, so color me blind.  The patch is not strictly code movement:

1.	In rcu_implicit_dynticks_qs(), it omits the "(curr & 0x1) =3D=3D
	0" from the test that determines whether or not the CPU passed
	through a quiescent state.  The reason I believe that this should
	be OK is that we don't get here if rdp->dynticks_snap had lower
	bit zero because in that case, the CPU would have been tagged
	by dyntick_save_progress_counter() as having been in a quiescent
	state.	So the inequality check subsumes the lower-bit-zero check.

2.	In print_cpu_stall_info(), an atomic_read() of the current counter
	is replaced by a call to rcu_dynticks_snap(), which does an atomic
	add of zero.  This adds overhead, but only during an RCU CPU stall
	warning, which I don't see one of in the dmesg.

3.	In print_one_rcu_data() an atomic_read() of the current counter
	is replaced by a call to rcu_dynticks_snap(), which does an atomic
	add of zero.  This adds overhead, but only when reading out
	debugfs trace data.  (Or does your test do this?)

Josh, could you please take another look to see what I am missing?

							Thanx, Paul

